WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.007 --> 00:00:14.210
headed quickly to the port to reserve three cabins on the Carnatic.

2
00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:18.252
He was delighted that Aouda was going to continue the journey with them.

3
00:00:19.173 --> 00:00:22.596
When he found Fix walking up and down the docks, he was not surprised.

4
00:00:24.577 --> 00:00:25.858
Fix seemed very worried.

5
00:00:26.719 --> 00:00:29.673
The detective had good reason to be upset— the warrant for Mr.

6
00:00:29.693 --> 00:00:31.174
Fogg's arrest had not arrived yet.

7
00:00:32.035 --> 00:00:34.237
It would not reach Hong Kong for two more days!

8
00:00:35.178 --> 00:00:37.500
Hong Kong was the last English territory on Mr.

9
00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:41.003
Fogg's route, and if Fix did not arrest him here, Fogg would escape.

10
00:00:41.023 --> 00:00:45.867
"Well, Monsieur Fix," said Passepartout as he caught up to the detective.

11
00:00:45.887 --> 00:00:48.909
"Have you decided to go with us as far as America?"

12
00:00:48.929 --> 00:00:52.612
"Yes," returned Fix through clenched teeth.

13
00:00:52.632 --> 00:00:56.436
"Good!" exclaimed Passepartout, laughing.

14
00:00:57.148 --> 00:00:59.230
"I knew you could not separate yourself from us.

15
00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:00.951
Come and purchase your ticket."

16
00:01:02.213 --> 00:01:06.056
Fix and Passepartout entered the steamer office and reserved four cabins.

17
00:01:07.157 --> 00:01:11.140
As the clerk handed them the tickets, he said that the repairs were complete on the Carnatic.

18
00:01:11.941 --> 00:01:15.904
The steamer would be ready to leave that evening, not the next morning as had been announced.

19
00:01:17.125 --> 00:01:20.148
"That will suit my master all the better," said Passepartout.

20
00:01:20.168 --> 00:01:21.889
"I will go and let him know."

21
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.692
Fix decided to make a bold move.

22
00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.282
It was time to tell Passepartout the truth about his master.

23
00:01:29.123 --> 00:01:31.725
He invited Passepartout to go to a tavern on the quay.

24
00:01:31.745 --> 00:01:37.009
"I really should inform my master of the steamer's change in schedule," said Passepartout.

25
00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:40.471
"We'll just have a quick drink," Fix promised.

26
00:01:40.491 --> 00:01:42.493
"I want to tell you something."

27
00:01:43.413 --> 00:01:46.996
"Well maybe just a quick one," Passepartout said with a smile.

28
00:01:48.857 --> 00:01:52.000
On entering the tavern, they noticed a large cot at the end of the room.

29
00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.082
Several people were lying on cushions and some were in a deep sleep.

30
00:01:59.744 --> 00:02:03.766
Passepartout realized, with mild surprise, that they were in an opium house.

31
00:02:04.586 --> 00:02:06.968
They ordered a bottle of wine and filled their glasses.

32
00:02:06.988 --> 00:02:10.349
"We must have a serious talk," said Fix.

33
00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:12.391
"A serious talk!"

34
00:02:12.831 --> 00:02:14.892
said Passepartout, emptying his glass.

35
00:02:14.912 --> 00:02:16.793
"Let's do that tomorrow.

36
00:02:16.893 --> 00:02:17.773
I don't have time now."

37
00:02:17.793 --> 00:02:21.916
"What I have to say concerns your master," said Fix.

38
00:02:23.008 --> 00:02:26.130
Passepartout looked at Fix, whose face had an odd expression.

39
00:02:27.011 --> 00:02:32.655
Fix placed his hand on Passepartout's arm and, lowering his voice, said, "You have guessed who I am?"

40
00:02:32.675 --> 00:02:34.456
"Of course!"

41
00:02:34.957 --> 00:02:36.398
said Passepartout, smiling.

42
00:02:36.418 --> 00:02:40.020
"Then I am going to tell you everything," Fix began.

43
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.683
"But I know everything already," said Passepartout.

44
00:02:44.283 --> 00:02:46.965
"Those gentlemen have put themselves to a useless expense."

45
00:02:48.426 --> 00:02:50.608
"Useless?" said Fix with surprise.

46
00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.281
"It's clear that you don't know how large the sum is."

47
00:02:54.301 --> 00:02:57.383
"Of course I do," said Passepartout.

48
00:02:58.003 --> 00:02:59.123
"Twenty-thousand pounds!"

49
00:02:59.143 --> 00:03:01.785
"Fifty-five thousand!"

50
00:03:02.185 --> 00:03:02.785
said Fix.

51
00:03:02.805 --> 00:03:05.366
"What!" cried Passepartout.

52
00:03:06.066 --> 00:03:09.468
"Fifty-five thousand pounds," said Fix.

53
00:03:09.488 --> 00:03:13.870
"Now, if you help me, I'll share my reward with you."

54
00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:15.110
"Help you?"

55
00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.551
cried Passepartout, his eyes wide open.

56
00:03:17.571 --> 00:03:20.348
"Yes, help me keep Mr.

57
00:03:20.428 --> 00:03:23.549
Fogg here for two or three days," said Fix.

58
00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:26.130
"It's a conspiracy then!

59
00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:30.151
And it is organized by the gentlemen of the Reform Club!"

60
00:03:30.431 --> 00:03:31.991
Passepartout was outraged.

61
00:03:32.012 --> 00:03:35.292
"Mr. Fix, my master is an honorable man.

62
00:03:36.093 --> 00:03:39.253
He is trying to win the wager fairly, and you are trying to postpone him.

63
00:03:40.034 --> 00:03:40.574
Shame on you!"

64
00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:43.835
The detective passed his hand across his forehead.

65
00:03:44.475 --> 00:03:46.515
He would have to break the news delicately.

66
00:03:47.836 --> 00:03:49.400
"Listen to me," said Fix.

67
00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:53.661
"I am a police detective, sent here by the London office.

68
00:03:54.682 --> 00:03:58.423
Mr. Fogg's wager is only a pretext for leaving the country.

69
00:03:59.463 --> 00:04:05.585
On September 28, a sum of fifty-five-thousand pounds was stolen from the Bank of England.

70
00:04:06.425 --> 00:04:08.345
Phileas Fogg is the bank robber."

71
00:04:09.106 --> 00:04:12.627
"Nonsense!" yelled Passepartout, slamming his fist on the table.

72
00:04:12.647 --> 00:04:15.747
"Would you like to be arrested as his partner?"

73
00:04:16.128 --> 00:04:17.408
asked Fix abruptly.

74
00:04:18.332 --> 00:04:19.993
"No!" said Passepartout.

75
00:04:20.013 --> 00:04:26.034
"I advise you to help me detain him, or you risk just such an arrest," said Fix.

76
00:04:26.054 --> 00:04:27.615
"I refuse!"

77
00:04:28.015 --> 00:04:28.995
said Passepartout.

78
00:04:29.655 --> 00:04:31.296
He held his head between his hands.

79
00:04:32.136 --> 00:04:33.576
He couldn't believe what he was hearing.

80
00:04:34.376 --> 00:04:35.857
He filled his glass again and drank.

81
00:04:38.658 --> 00:04:40.898
Fix put his arm around Passepartout's shoulder.

82
00:04:40.918 --> 00:04:46.960
"If that is how you feel," he said in a companionable way, "please forget that I have said

83
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:53.297
anything." Some pipes full of opium lay on the table, and Fix slipped one into Passepartout's hand.

84
00:04:54.298 --> 00:05:00.480
Passepartout drew several puffs, and he fell unconscious on the table.

85
00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:06.221
While Fix was delaying Passepartout at the opium house, Mr.

86
00:05:06.261 --> 00:05:08.942
Fogg was escorting Aouda around the shops of Hong Kong.

87
00:05:09.883 --> 00:05:10.883
A lady, as Mr.

88
00:05:10.903 --> 00:05:16.116
Fogg was well aware, could not be expected to travel with only a carpetbag.

89
00:05:16.136 --> 00:05:18.777
In the evening they returned to the hotel for an elegant dinner.

90
00:05:19.677 --> 00:05:22.118
At bedtime Passepartout still had not returned.

91
00:05:22.918 --> 00:05:26.339
If Phileas Fogg could have been astonished by anything, this might have done it.

92
00:05:27.599 --> 00:05:31.820
But, thinking that the Carnatic was not departing until the morning, he was not concerned.

93
00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.061
The next morning Mr.

94
00:05:34.101 --> 00:05:38.662
Fogg and Aouda prepared their bags and arrived at the quay to learn that the Carnatic had sailed

95
00:05:38.682 --> 00:05:39.602
the evening before.

96
00:05:40.422 --> 00:05:41.603
Upon hearing the news, Mr.

97
00:05:41.623 --> 00:05:44.784
Fogg's comment to Aouda was simply, "It is an accident, madam.

98
00:05:45.424 --> 00:05:45.904
Nothing more."

99
00:05:49.728 --> 00:05:52.410
Well, there I was, asleep in an opium den!

100
00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:55.372
That Detective Fix, I hope I never see him again!

101
00:05:56.413 --> 00:05:59.495
Perhaps you had never heard of an opium den before reading about our adventures.

102
00:06:00.355 --> 00:06:04.718
Now you know that these dens are like bars where the drug opium is served and smoked through

103
00:06:04.758 --> 00:06:09.321
pipes. Beds are provided for people to lie down since opium makes people sleepy.

104
00:06:10.542 --> 00:06:13.184
Opium is a highly addictive drug made from poppies.

105
00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:18.121
It was used as a medicine in China starting in the fifteenth century but was banned in the

106
00:06:18.221 --> 00:06:20.282
eighteenth century due to its harmful effects.

107
00:06:21.302 --> 00:06:25.904
Despite the ban opium use in China flourished, and many Chinese people became addicted.

108
00:06:26.744 --> 00:06:32.186
That was because a powerful British company called the East India Company started growing opium in India and

109
00:06:32.326 --> 00:06:34.427
smuggling it into China in huge amounts!

110
00:06:35.607 --> 00:06:38.708
This business was so profitable that the British government let it continue.

111
00:06:39.604 --> 00:06:43.966
The Chinese went to war with the British twice to stop the smuggling, but the British won both

112
00:06:44.006 --> 00:06:48.609
times. As Chinese people emigrated around the world, they brought opium with them.

113
00:06:49.429 --> 00:06:53.291
Soon opium dens spread to large cities such as London and San Francisco.
